rpresser wrote:
> As I said above: Clicking with the mouse is not meant to move you one
> space in that direction. Clicking with the mouse is meant to "Travel"
> you to the position you clicked: to take the shortest path from where
> you are to where you clicked, stopping only if something "interesting"
> is encountered (a monster, a door, some writing, an object on the
> floor, etc.)

      Clicking next to your position acts as an ordinary single
step move (rather than using travel mode to get to that position).
Double-clicking next to your position acts as a shift move and
runs until you bump into something.  "I click three times in a
row" is evidently happening rapidly enough for two of those
consecutive clicks to be interpreted as a double click.

      "Doctor, it hurts when I do _this_."...

      Travel mode via mouse click can be turned off via the 'O'
command or OPTIONS=noTravel, but having double clicks be treated
as shift move cannot.  The timing involved in deciding whether
two clicks constitute a double click is probably something you
can tweak within the operating system or windowing system; it's
not something that you can manipulate within nethack.


